Purpose and intent.
The ES District is intended to provide suitable areas for utility, institutional, conservation, public safety and other facilities that are essential to the public health, safety, welfare and economic stability of the city.
Permitted uses.
1. Colleges and Universities;
2. Community Centers;
3. Courthouses;
4. Data Centers;
5. Electric substations;
6. Government administrative offices;
7. Information product research and development;
8. Institutions of higher learning, business colleges, music conservatories and similar institutions;
9. Laboratories (medical and dental);
10. Libraries;
11. Medical offices (excluding veterinary);
12. Parks;
13. Public safety buildings, grounds and services;
14. Public utility facilities;
15. Religious institutions;
16. Schools;
17. Solar, renewable and alternative energy facilities;
18. Transit support site (i.e. park and ride, bus areas, charging stations);
19. Telecommunication offices (excluding towers).
Conditional uses permitted by mayor and city council.
1. Telecommunication towers and antennas - See standards in article 7, division 1.
Accessory structures.
1. When an accessory building is attached to the principal building in any manner, it shall be deemed part of the principal structure and subject to all bulk and area requirements of the same.
2. Guard houses and security entry features shall be permitted at public road entrances.
Use limitations.
1. All outside storage areas must be located in the rear yard and must be screened by an opaque fence, wall, shrubbery or trees no less than eight feet in height.
2. Air conditioning units and HVAC systems shall be thoroughly screened from view from the public right-of-way and from adjacent properties by using walls, fencing, roof elements, or landscaping. This requirement shall not apply where the equipment is more than 100 feet from adjacent property lines.
3. The front building facade of all principal buildings shall be oriented toward street fronts or adjacent arterial street fronts. This requirement shall not apply if the front of the building is greater than 500 feet from the public right-of-way or not visible from the public right-of-way.
4. Areas used for parking of any vehicle or trailer in this district shall be finished with a surface treated and hardened with concrete, asphalt, porous pavers, tar and gravel mix or the like.
5. Exterior illumination shall be shielded, downcast, and of a luminosity designed to maintain the existing night sky darkness and to prevent light trespass onto adjacent properties. In order to obtain that objective, the following criteria shall be met:
a. All fixtures shall be full cut-off type fixtures;
b. Light poles shall be no taller than 25 feet in height;
c. All light poles must be setback a minimum of 10 feet from any exterior property line;
d. Maximum foot-candles at the property line shall be 0.5;
e. The zoning administrator may require a photometric lighting plan which shows conformity with these requirements as part of any building permit application.
6. Noise-level standards:
a. Facility operations shall not produce continuous sound that exceeds an average of 65 decibels over any 30-minute period from 8:00 A.M. to 6:00 P.M., measured at any adjacent property boundary between any noise emitting structure or equipment and an adjacent property boundary. Nor operations produce continuous sound that exceeds an average of 55 decibels over any thirty-minute period from 6:00 P.M. to 8:00 A.M. measured at any adjacent property boundary. Violations of these sound levels may be prosecuted in the same manner as other zoning ordinance violations.
b. After issuance of the Certificate of Occupancy for each building, the city may obtain sound studies or require the property owner to provide a sound study to verify that the operation is in compliance with the requirements of paragraph (a) above. If the results of the study indicate a violation of the requirements of paragraph (a) above, the city may issue a notice of violation, which may direct that the owner to take appropriate steps to operate within the requirements of paragraph (a) above. The zoning administrator may require the owner to propose a solution and time period for implementation. If the zoning administrator approves such a solution, and the owner fails to successfully implement that solution within the time approved, the violator shall be subject to a fine up to $1,000.00 for each day that the violation exists until full compliance is obtained.
c. The property owner shall continue to bear the costs of any sound test or study required to monitor violations in paragraph (b).
Bulk and area regulations.
1. Minimum lot size: 40,000 square feet.
2. Minimum lot frontage: 50 feet.
3. Minimum lot width at building line: 50 feet.
4. Maximum building height: 75 feet.
a. The height limitations do not apply to structures such as unoccupied and inaccessible architectural features on commercial or institutional buildings (e.g., church spires, church belfries, cupolas and domes on commercial buildings), government-owned observation towers, water towers, chimneys, smokestacks, conveyors, flag poles, masts, aerials, and similar structures, except that none of the above shall, within a three mile radius of the Cartersville-Bartow County Airport, exceed a height above the "clear zone" required for a safe approach to said airport as set forth by the Federal Aviation Administration.
5. Maximum impervious surface: 80 percent.
6. Minimum landscaped area: ten percent.
7. Front setback: 50 feet.
8. Side setback: 20 feet.
9. Rear setback: 50 feet.
10. Minimum parking required: one space per 300 square feet.
a. For the purposes of parking calculations the square footage is based on the net occupiable floor area of the building to include officers, corridors, restrooms, locker rooms, break rooms, conference rooms, security stations and other similar support spaces but does not include mechanical closets, riser rooms, server rackspace, battery rooms, data halls and other areas dedicated primarily to equipment or building infrastructure.
Landscape and buffer requirements.
1. When a property in this district directly abuts a residential district, a 20 foot greenbelt buffer shall be established. Such buffer must include a solid fence, wall, shrubbery or trees no less than six feet in height.
2. Landscaped areas must contain appropriate materials such as grass, hedges, trees, natural vegetation and the like. Landscaping along rights-of-way and within parking lots may be counted within this figure, however, no more than 50 percent of the required landscaping provided may be within stormwater retention facilities.
3. Stormwater retention facilities are not permitted within required buffers.
(Ord. No. 2025-009, § 1D, 6-9-2025)

